        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Decembrist revolt</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, where the Northern Society met and the rising took place in Senate Square; Tulchin and Vasylkiv in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, centres of the Southern Society; Novohrad-Volynsky, where the Society of United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> was founded; afterwards places of exile across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Union of Salvation from 1816; Union of Prosperity, suspended in 1821; Northern and Southern Societies 1821–1825; Society of United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> from 1823; the rising in Senate Square in December 1825; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> Regiment’s revolt in January 1826; trial and exile from 1826; amnesty 1856</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Pavel Pestel, radical leader of the Southern Society; Nikita Muravyov, author of a constitution; Prince Sergei Trubetskoy, chosen as interim ruler, who deserted the rising; Prince Eugene Obolensky, who took his place; Sergey Muravyov-Apostol, leader of the revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>; Pyotr Kakhovsky; Maria Volkonskaya, who followed her husband into exile</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">crushed by artillery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>; five leaders hanged and many others imprisoned or exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a> continued for almost a century, but the revolt has been considered the beginning of a revolutionary movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a></span></div>
