    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Officers who had seen the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> and the suffering of peasant soldiers formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> from 1816, seeking a constitution and the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</li><li>In the confusion after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>’s death in 1825 they led about 3,000 troops into Senate Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, where loyal troops broke them with grapeshot at the end of the day.</li><li>Five leaders were hanged and many exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrists</a> founded schools and brought new crops, and their wives’ devotion made the word Dekabristka a Russian symbol.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The rising failed partly because its chosen leader, Prince Sergei Trubetskoy, deserted it on the day, and his second-in-command, Colonel Bulatov, also vanished from the scene; the rebels had to appoint Prince Eugene Obolensky in a hurried consultation.</p></aside>
    </section>

